cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this paper or its Lean 4 verification artifact, please cite it as below."
title: "Formal Falsification of the Completeness of a Single Fixed-Memory Component for Open Inversion Tasks"
abstract: >-
  This paper asks whether a single fixed-memory component can be complete, on its own, for open
  inversion tasks: inverse problems whose observations are ill-posed, whose required context is
  heterogeneous and addressed by the evidence, and whose inference inputs are not constrained by the
  training distribution. The analysis distinguishes values instantiated from evidence or analytic
  invariants (Mode A) from values frozen at inference from corpus statistics or manual setting (Mode B).
  For mask-modeled inputs, under a fixed test distribution, integrable squared loss, a fixed
  evidence-instantiated benchmark, a specified offline-frozen class with a uniform bounded-description
  rate, and both barrier and scale-restricted realization conditions, the paper proves a conditional
  risk gap kappa = p delta^2 / 16 - risk*; when kappa > 0, no rule in that class achieves uniform
  tolerance 0 < epsilon < kappa. The neural corollary additionally assumes output variation on an
  evidence-collision pair. The result limits solo use; it does not deny the usefulness of frozen
  components in composite systems, and it does not establish BD, P4, or real-world applicability. The
  algebraic core and a toy closure are formalized in Lean 4 with Mathlib, without sorry. This repository
  holds the JMLR LaTeX source, the compiled paper, and the Lean 4 artifact.
